t the containers configuration and document what's needed for production instead. clean\n- Cons: Containers not wired yet\n\n**Option B**: Fix the wrangler.toml to have the correct CF Containers format and create Container class stubs\n- Pros: More complete implementation\n- Cons: Complex, might not work in local dev anyway\n\n**Option C**: Move the `[[containers]]` sections to a comment with documentation about how to enable them for production\n- Same as Option A\n\nI'll go with Option A/C: comment out the containers sections and add clear instructions. The `SIDECAR_SERVICE?: Fetcher` and `PLUGIN_CONTAINER?: Fetcher` in env.ts remain optional, so the fallback works. When the user wants to enable CF Containers, they can uncomment and set up the Container classes.\n\nActually, let me think through the CF Containers API more carefully. Cloudflare Workers Containers (2025) lets you run Docker containers alongside your Worker, either through Container Instances with their own lifecycle that you communicate with via fetch, or through Service Bindings that connect to a container like another Worker.\n\nThe error about `binding` being unexpected tells me that `[[containers]]` in wrangler.toml uses `class_name` instead. The real issue is that I need to remove the `binding` field, add the proper `class_name` field, set up Durable Object bindings for the containers, export the Container classes from the Worker, and update env.ts accordingly.\n\nBut this would require creating Container classes and potentially changing the fetch API. Let me figure out the actual API from Cloudflare's announcement: Containers are Durable Objects-powered, so the env type would be `DurableObjectNamespace`, and I'd use the pattern of getting an ID, getting a stub, then calling fetch on it.\n\nWait — DurableObjectStubs do have a `.fetch()` method, so the existing code pattern might actually work if I just wire up the bindings correctly.\n\nLet me map out the changes needed: I need to update wrangler.toml to add class names and Durable Object bindings for the containers, export Container subclasses in the Worker code, update the env types to use `DurableObjectNamespace` instead of `Fetcher`, and then modify the workflow code to get stubs from the namespace before calling fetch.\n\nActually, I'm realizing the CF Containers API might use a different type like `ContainerApp` depending on the Cloudflare Workers version, and containers won't work in local development anyway since they require Docker. So I can stub out the container classes to throw errors and rely on the KV fallback for local dev. Given the time constraints, the most practical approach is to comment out the container sections in wrangler.toml for now with clear notes on how to re-enable them when deploying with CF Containers, while keeping the optional bindings in place. I need the next thinking to rewrite. Please provide the thinking that comes after the current rewritten thinking you've shown me.",
